  "question": {
    "text": "A capacitance of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2&#160;</mn><mi>&#956;F</mi></math> is required in an electrical circuit across a potential difference of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mtext>.</mtext><mn>0</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kV</mi></math>. A large number of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi>&#956;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></math> capacitors are available which can withstand a potential difference of not more than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mn>0</mn><mn>0</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>. The minimum number of capacitors required to achieve this is:",
    "image": null
  },
